WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:09.023
The letter that Eponine had delivered was addressed to Marius at 16 Rue de la Verrerie. He broke the seal and read it:

2
00:00:09.024 --> 00:00:20.856
My beloved, Alas, my father wishes us to leave immediately. Tonight we will stay at 7 Rue de l’Homme Armé. In a week we will be in England. Cosette

3
00:00:20.857 --> 00:00:29.610
Marius was unaware of how Eponine had come to have this letter. She had been as much in love with Marius as he was with Cosette.

4
00:00:29.611 --> 00:00:37.864
Determined to keep Marius and Cosette apart, Eponine had borrowed clothes from a young man and warned Valjean to move out.

5
00:00:37.865 --> 00:00:44.999
After receiving the mysterious warning, Valjean had immediately announced to Cosette and Toussaint that they were leaving.

6
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.406
Cosette needed to write to Marius, but she couldn’t take the letter herself.

7
00:00:49.407 --> 00:00:55.836
Fortunately she noticed the young man in velvet trousers loitering outside the garden gate.

8
00:00:55.837 --> 00:01:00.678
Cosette gave the letter to "him," but Eponine did not deliver it.

9
00:01:00.679 --> 00:01:06.124
Now Marius covered Cosette’s letter with kisses. She still loved him!

10
00:01:06.125 --> 00:01:10.995
For a moment it seemed that he did not have to die. But then he remembered.

11
00:01:10.996 --> 00:01:18.387
"Her father is taking her to England, and my grandfather refuses to consent to our marriage," said Marius to himself.

12
00:01:18.388 --> 00:01:20.400
"Nothing has changed."

13
00:01:20.401 --> 00:01:28.725
So he had only two duties left. The first was to write to Cosette one last time and inform her of his certain death.

14
00:01:28.726 --> 00:01:35.058
The second duty was to save Eponine’s brother, Gavroche, by getting him away from the barricade.

15
00:01:35.059 --> 00:01:38.375
Marius took a pencil and paper from his pocket.

16
00:01:38.376 --> 00:01:40.267
My dear Cosette:

17
00:01:40.268 --> 00:01:48.550
We cannot be married. I have asked my grandfather for permission, and he has refused me as you and I are both poor.

18
00:01:48.551 --> 00:01:55.875
I ran to your house in the Rue Plumet, but did not find you there. Do you remember my promise that I would die if you went away?

19
00:01:55.876 --> 00:02:04.556
I will keep it, and I will die. I love you. When you read this, my soul will be near you and will smile upon you.

20
00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:06.265
Marius

21
00:02:06.266 --> 00:02:12.538
He did not have an envelope, so he merely folded the letter and wrote Cosette’s address upon it.

22
00:02:12.539 --> 00:02:18.363
Marius stood lost in thought for a moment. Then he took out another piece of paper and wrote:

23
00:02:18.364 --> 00:02:27.919
My name is Marius Pontmercy. Carry my corpse to my grandfather’s house: Monsieur Gillenormand, 6 Rue des Filles du Calvaire.

24
00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:33.261
He put this note in his pocket, and then he called Eponine’s brother, Gavroche.

25
00:02:33.262 --> 00:02:36.473
"Will you do something for me?" asked Marius.

26
00:02:36.474 --> 00:02:40.657
"Of course I will. You saved my life."

27
00:02:40.658 --> 00:02:42.814
"Leave the barricade immediately.

28
00:02:42.815 --> 00:02:49.562
Then take this letter first thing tomorrow morning to Mademoiselle Cosette at 7 Rue de l’Homme Armé."

29
00:02:49.563 --> 00:02:55.665
"But I want to be here when the soldiers attack the barricade again. Can’t I leave tomorrow morning?"

30
00:02:55.666 --> 00:03:02.070
"No, it’ll be too late. The streets will be blockaded and you won’t be able to get out. Go right away!"

31
00:03:02.071 --> 00:03:06.657
Gavroche was still puzzled, but he took the letter and scampered away.

32
00:03:06.658 --> 00:03:14.562
"That street is not far away," said Gavroche to himself. "I can deliver the letter and return in time for the action."

33
00:03:14.563 --> 00:03:20.229
Meanwhile Jean Valjean had calmed down as soon as he reached the Rue de l’Homme Armé.

34
00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:28.213
Cosette had not left the Rue Plumet without resistance. It was the first time that her will had clashed with his.

35
00:03:28.214 --> 00:03:31.058
They arrived at their new home without speaking.

36
00:03:31.059 --> 00:03:39.364
Valjean was so anxious that he didn’t notice Cosette’s sadness, and she was so sad that she didn’t perceive his anxiety.

37
00:03:39.365 --> 00:03:47.626
In their flight from the Rue Plumet, Valjean had taken only a small bundle of clothes and the small suitcase he carried everywhere.

38
00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:52.807
A trunk would have required the help of another man, and another man might talk.

39
00:03:52.808 --> 00:04:00.864
Toussaint and Cosette took a few clothes and toilet articles. Cosette also took her portable writing desk and its blotter.

40
00:04:00.865 --> 00:04:05.685
The next morning Cosette said that she had a headache and spent the day in bed.

41
00:04:05.686 --> 00:04:10.189
Toward evening she came downstairs, but soon returned to her room.

42
00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:15.991
While Valjean ate a small supper, he became aware that Toussaint was agitated.

43
00:04:15.992 --> 00:04:20.015
"What’s wrong?" he finally asked the old servant.

44
00:04:20.016 --> 00:04:27.753
"I’m not sure what’s going on," she said. "But there seems to be a battle taking place in the city."

45
00:04:27.754 --> 00:04:32.428
"Oh, really?" he said absently, more concerned with his own affairs.

46
00:04:32.429 --> 00:04:39.710
After supper he felt better and walked around the dining room, still filled with the bundles that Toussaint had packed for them.

47
00:04:39.711 --> 00:04:47.079
Suddenly he spotted something strange. Cosette had left her writing desk and blotter on the sideboard when she arrived.

48
00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:53.275
The blotter was reflected in the sideboard’s mirror; he could now read the last letter that she had blotted.

49
00:04:53.276 --> 00:04:55.896
It was the letter to Marius.

50
00:04:55.897 --> 00:05:03.126
Valjean was stunned. Cosette was in love with a man, and she had told him about their move to England.

51
00:05:03.127 --> 00:05:06.314
"She plans to leave me!" Valjean whispered.

52
00:05:06.316 --> 00:05:09.134
But who could this young man be?

53
00:05:09.135 --> 00:05:14.565
It did not take Valjean long to realize that it must be the one from the Luxembourg Gardens.

54
00:05:14.566 --> 00:05:18.547
There was no other man with whom Cosette had been in contact.

55
00:05:18.548 --> 00:05:25.785
Valjean was so upset that he did not know what he was doing. He went outside and sat on the steps.

56
00:05:25.786 --> 00:05:32.110
That is what he was doing when Gavroche approached him and asked, "Can you tell me where number seven is?"

57
00:05:32.111 --> 00:05:35.213
"What do you want with that house?" asked Valjean.

58
00:05:35.214 --> 00:05:41.602
Gavroche hesitated, but Valjean suddenly had an idea of why the boy might be there.

59
00:05:41.603 --> 00:05:44.142
"Have you brought the letter I’m waiting for?"

60
00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:48.023
"It’s not for you. It’s for a woman."

61
00:05:48.024 --> 00:05:53.681
"The letter is for Mademoiselle Cosette, isn’t it? She told me to wait here for it."

62
00:05:53.682 --> 00:06:01.506
"Yes." Gavroche handed over the letter. "Tell her it came from the barricade at the Rue de la Chanvrerie."

63
00:06:01.507 --> 00:06:07.929
Valjean’s heart was filled with malice toward Marius; he wanted to kill his daughter’s suitor!

64
00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:11.991
The old man waited until he was inside the house to read the letter.

65
00:06:11.992 --> 00:06:15.242
As he read it, his anger turned to joy.

66
00:06:15.243 --> 00:06:23.245
There was no need to commit murder because Marius was probably already dead. Cosette would not leave Valjean after all!

67
00:06:23.246 --> 00:06:26.949
And yet Valjean was also filled with gloom.

68
00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:34.808
Cosette would never know what had happened to Marius. Valjean must try to save him if only for her sake.

69
00:06:34.809 --> 00:06:43.388
For many years Valjean had served as a member of the National Guard. Before dawn he went out, armed and in his uniform.

70
00:06:43.389 --> 00:06:51.396
Meanwhile Marius had given his role as leader of the rebels to Enjolras, who ordered that the dead be removed.

71
00:06:51.397 --> 00:06:57.634
Four National Guards had been killed; Enjolras had their uniforms laid aside in a pile.

72
00:06:57.635 --> 00:07:00.975
There were 37 rebels still alive.

73
00:07:00.976 --> 00:07:07.102
Enjolras went out on a scouting mission. When he returned, he had grim news.

74
00:07:07.103 --> 00:07:13.951
"A third of the army is advancing toward us. As for the people, they have abandoned us.

75
00:07:13.952 --> 00:07:21.102
We have four National Guard uniforms here, so four of you can escape. There’s no need for everyone to die."

76
00:07:21.103 --> 00:07:23.720
No one wanted to leave the barricade.

77
00:07:23.721 --> 00:07:30.943
It was finally decided that those with families should go. But that meant five men now wanted to leave.

78
00:07:30.944 --> 00:07:38.852
Marius looked up and saw Cosette’s father entering the barricade, dressed in his National Guard uniform.

